    <description>
      <Value><![CDATA[{
  "blocks": {
    "contentData": [],
    "settingsData": [],
    "expose": [],
    "Layout": {}
  },
  "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EDevelopment proposals \u003Cstrong\u003Eshould \u003C/strong\u003Eincorporate measures to support bat conservation by providing suitable roosting and foraging opportunities. This can be achieved by: \u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003Eintegrating permanent bat roost features, such as bat bricks, bat tiles and/or roost tubes, into the walls or roof spaces of buildings. These \u003Cstrong\u003Eshould \u003C/strong\u003Ebe positioned at soffit level, oriented south to west or south east to maximize solar warmth, and located away from windows, doors, and primary access routes \u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003Einstalling durable woodcrete or hardwood bat boxes on mature trees (where an integrated approach is not feasible), ideally 3 to 6 metres above ground with clear flight paths. Boxes \u003Cstrong\u003Eshould \u003C/strong\u003Ebe mounted using coach bolts to avoid damage to trees \u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003Eenhancing the surrounding landscape to support foraging by planting native hedgerows (e.g. hawthorn, hazel, holly) or larger native trees, as the structure of the habitat is more critical than the specific species \u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Cp\u003EFurther technical guidance can be found on the Bat Conservation Trust webpage about bat boxes.\u003C/p\u003E"
}]]></Value>
    </description>
